.Xc
From now on also write what the program in the pane prints to the standard
input of
.Ar shell-command
(a format, run with
.Pa /bin/sh ) ;
without a command, stop.
With -I what the command prints is typed into the pane instead (-I -O: both
ways); it is read only while the pane's input queue (1 MiB) has room.
A pane has one pipe: a new command replaces the old.
With -o a pipe that is open is only closed, so one key can toggle it.
At most 1 MiB waits for a command that reads slowly; output beyond that is
not piped.
