Rotate the panes of a window (-D: the other way).
.It Xo
.Ic run-shell Pq Ic run
.Op Fl bCE
.Op Fl c Ar start-directory
.Op Fl d Ar delay
.Op Fl t Ar target-pane
.Op Ar shell-command
.Xc
Run
.Ar shell-command
(a format) with
.Pa /bin/sh
and print its output; a command that fails also prints
.Dq 'command' returned N
and its status becomes the exit status of
.Nm .
The commands after run-shell wait for it unless -b is given.
-E includes standard error, -d waits
.Ar delay
seconds first (a fraction is allowed), -c sets the directory, and with -C
.Ar shell-command
is a list of
.Nm
commands.
The command finds this server through
.Ev LIF
and its target pane in
.Ev LIF_PANE .
.It Xo
.Ic select-layout Pq Ic selectl
.Op Fl Enop
.Op Fl t Ar target-pane
.Oo Ar layout-name Oc
.Xc
Apply a layout: even-horizontal, even-vertical, main-horizontal,
main-vertical, main-horizontal-mirrored, main-vertical-mirrored, tiled, or a
layout string. -E spreads panes evenly, -n and -p step through presets, -o
undoes the last layout change.
.It Xo
.Ic select-pane Pq Ic selectp
.Op Fl DdeLlMmRUZ
.Op Fl T Ar title
.Op Fl t Ar target-pane
.Xc
Make a pane active, or the pane in a direction with -L, -R, -U, -D; -l
selects the last pane. -T sets the pane's title and selects nothing; a
title the program sets later replaces it. -d turns the pane's input off
(what clients type and paste there is dropped) and -e on again.
-m marks the pane, or clears the mark if the pane has it, and -M clears it;
neither selects anything.
The marked pane is the default
.Ar src-pane
of join-pane and swap-pane and the default
.Ar src-window
of swap-window.
.It Xo
.Ic select-window Pq Ic selectw
.Op Fl lnpT
.Op Fl t Ar target-window
.Xc
Select a window; -l last, -n next, -p previous, -T toggles to the last
window if already current.
